{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793531498,"nonce":"bWaxgJ+2rLQCN5+UwhOcU/j98/E=","message":"Owner decision 2026-11-01 (ADR-0077 grill): BUILD route-by-cache-key now. Not waiting for a measured hit rate: N Depot replicas must not look broken to a potential customer, and `just chaos` (2 Depots) exercises it.\n\nAudit (2026-11-01) correction to this ticket: a saved cache tree does NOT live whole on one replica. Cache-only PUTs scatter like every drain PUT (16-wide through the ClusterIP, scarab-workspace-client lib.rs:64; cache dirs are cache-only, lib.rs:1982-1990, workspaced.rs:2602-2618). So a per-entry \"replica hint\" cannot work: route the save as well as the restore.\n\nDesign (chosen over peer-fill-by-owner, ~700-1000 LOC touching every read arm, and durable caches, which reverse the warm-only decision):\n- The executor computes R = rendezvous(cache_key, Depot replica count) and gives the Pod a second Depot URL, R's stable name through a new headless Service, for cache-dir content only.\n- The drain helper sends cache-dir blobs/trees to R (needs a cache-dir label distinct from CacheOnly, which also covers a pruned `outputs:` remainder); everything else keeps the ClusterIP. A save that cannot reach R falls back to the ClusterIP (a later miss, never a failed Step).\n- wsfetch restores cache roots from R. R down, evicted or remapped by a scale change = a miss.\n- Plug points: executor-k8s lib.rs ~526 and ~3931; wsfetch restore_cache; the drain helper's label plan.\n- Depends on the cache-root walk fix (filed separately) — without it N\u003e1 with caches can fail required fetches.\nEstimate ~250-350 LOC, 4-5 tests. Rank 1 chaos at 2+2 must show cache hits after the change.","files":null}]}